Optimal Solution - Uses cycle detection with a hash set to determine whether a number is happy.
                   Either the process reaches 1, or it loops—use a set to detect repetition and stop early.

class Solution {
    public boolean isHappy(int n) {
        // Set to track numbers already seen to detect cycles
        Set<Integer> usedInt = new HashSet<>();
        // Repeat until number becomes 1 or a cycle is detected
        while (true) {
            int sum = 0;
            // Compute sum of squares of digits
            while (n != 0) {
                int digit = n % 10;
                sum += digit * digit; // square of digit
                n /= 10;
            }
            // If we reach 1, it's a happy number
            if (sum == 1) return true;
            // Prepare for next iteration
            n = sum;
            // If we've seen this number before, a cycle exists
            if (usedInt.contains(n)) {
                return false;
            }
            // Mark the number as seen
            usedInt.add(n);
        }
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(log n).
Space Complexity :- O(log n).
